I. INTRODUCTION
T HE Superconducting FRagment Separator (Super-FRS) of the Facility for Anti-proton and Ion Research (FAIR [1]) which is currently being built in Darmstadt (Germany) will provide intense secondary particle beams of unstable nuclei or antiprotons to three accelerator branches: the ring, the high and the low energy branches. While entering the low energy branch, the ion beam goes through the S-shape energy buncher (EB) which, combined to the Super-FRS, provides a high-resolution dispersion-matched spectrometer allowing to run diverse sets of heavy ion nuclear physics experiments [2].
Several years ago [3], [4], CEA provided preliminary designs for the Super-FRS 24 superconducting dipoles which are currently being manufactured [5] by Elytt Energy [6] and tested at CERN [7]. To pursue the fruitful synergy between FAIR and the CEA team, CEA is now in charge of the design of the three iron-dominated superconducting dipoles, D1, D2 and D3 shown in Fig. 1. The design of the superferric dipoles of
