III. MECHANICAL ALIGNMENT AND CRYOGENIC DESIGN
The main aspects of the CEA design (see Fig. 2) are tunable and trackable (alignment) tie rods supporting the cold coil casing (4.5 K) from the room temperature vacuum vessel structure, active thermosiphon for the coil cooling scheme and tailored yoke geometry (slits and custom end chamfers) to fulﬁll the homogeneity requirements over a broad range of operating currents as indicated in Table I. Note that the latter is developed in Section IV.
A. Mechanical Alignment
When coils and their coil casing (CC) are enclosed in the cryostat, one needs to have the ability to check if they are well centered inside to avoid any thermal short circuit between the different components of the cryostat: cold mass at 4 K, thermal shield at 80 K, multiple layer insulation (30 layers), vacuum vessel (VV) at 300 K.
Fig. 3 shows the eight tie rods (4 horizontal and 4 vertical) with their alignment nests. Alignment nests are added at the tip of the tie rods in order to measure their position with respect to the reference frame of the cryostat with a laser tracker. Adding these surveying capabilities during the assembly of the ﬁrst Super-FRS

