Once the cryostat is completely assembled, the algorithm described previously cannot be used as the CC is not accessible. For ﬁnal veriﬁcation purpose, it is however possible to retrieve the set of rotations and translations of the CC which is compatible with the surveyed location of the 8 tie rod tips. It is not as straightforward as previously but this could be done through genetic algorithm in which population of individuals characterized by 3 rotations and 3 translations are evaluated by comparing the ﬁnal position of their 8 tips with respect to the surveyed ones.
B. Cryogenic Design
1) Cooling system: As Super-FRS dipoles [15], the coils of EB dipoles are cooled by two helium channels grooved in the coil casing. The mass ﬂow of diphasic helium at 4.5 K and 1.3 bar is generated using the thermosiphon principle, each coil having its own independent hydraulic circuit. On the contrary of Super-FRS dipoles, the helium channels, which have a rectangular shape (12 mm × 20 mm), are both located above the coils (see Fig. 4). This naturally brings the vapor in the upper part of the channels, away from coils, inducing a better wetting by liquid helium. Since the channels are horizontal and connected to the helium tank vertically, the thermosiphon is made “active” by adding a heater on the return pipe of each circuit: this forces the mass ﬂow direction and increases it if necessary. This concept has been validated experimentally by a R&D experiment done at Saclay[16] and by the cold tests of the FoS D2 Super-FRS dipole at CERN [5].
2) Thermal budget: The heat loads on the cold mass and the thermal shield are obtained by the sum of the radiation and conduction heat loads. The different conduction loads are: through the 8 cold-to-warm supports, through the 44 spacers between the thermal shield and the cold mass and through the safety and the current leads tubes which are between the helium tank and the outside of the vacuum vessel at room temperature. For the copper made thermal shield, the load is removed by the mass ﬂow of helium gas which is between 55 K and 75 K through pipes and plates brazed and glued on the copper. For the cold mass, the heat load is removed by the mass ﬂow of liquid helium in the helium channels of the casing. For the 4 vertical tie rods,

it must be noted that the non intercepted heat load goes directly to the helium ﬂow since the helium channel of the upper coil is between the coil and the contact surface of the casing. The different heat loads are summarized in Table II.
3) Safety devices: The dipoles are protected from overpressure by two safety devices: a safety valve that opens at 20 bar (pressure design of the cold mass) and a burst disc that should open at 25 bar in the event that the safety valve fails to open. These devices are mounted on a manifold itself connected to the safety tube. For sizing the safety devices, the worst scenario taken into account is the loss of vacuum of the vacuum vessel that induces a quench of the magnet. The calculation has been done in two ways. First, an energy balance on the helium has been carried out, taking also into consideration the heat exchange between the safety tube and the expelled cold helium: this allows to have the dynamic evolution of the temperature and of the pressure. This yields a safety valve with a minimum diameter of 32.5 mm and 27.5 mm of diameter for the burst disc. Both allow to not exceeding the Maximum Allowable Pressure that is 110 % of the design pressure and the maximum pressure drop of 3 % allowed in the applicable standard in the safety tube. At the same time, using the more conservative solution from the EN21013-3 and EN17527 standards, the minimum dimensions of safety valve is 40 mm in diameter and 35 mm for the burst disc diameter which are conservative values that must be used to comply with the European Pressure Equipment Directive 2014/68/EU.
IV. ELECTROMAGNETIC ANALYSIS
A. Magnetic Homogeneity
Unfortunately, it was not possible to transpose the single-slit solution obtained for the Super-FRS dipoles. This is a direct consequence of the three times bigger bending angle and the shorter bending radius which makes the distribution of yoke saturation varies more across the aperture over the range of operating currents. Additional degrees of freedom for the optimization were added by giving the possibility to the 2D algorithm to increase the number of air slits in the central pole. The 2D algorithm developed here is a genetic algorithm in Python coupled to Opera 2D magnetic simulation software. The Python algorithm randomly deﬁnes the population of 2D yoke geometries with different sets of slits position and size, then each individual of the population is evaluated with the Opera2D model. For the evaluation, the BH-curve is tuned to provide a yoke saturation in the 2D return legs similar to the one of the 3D model at the foreseen nominal current (≈ 230 A). The longitudinal ﬁeld integral is computed as if the 3D geometry was made of the

