This allows to compute a ﬁeld integral over a theoretical arc (4.375 m radius) and evaluate the homogeneity, deﬁned in [15], over the elliptical good ﬁeld region. As a result, introducing three slits (see Table III) provides a good compromise between homogeneity performance and magnet efﬁciency. Fig. 5 shows the homogeneity obtained from 3D simulations at nominal current after an adjustment of the end chamfers geometry, any uncolored area (white) indicates an homogeneity beyond the ±1.5 10−4 criteria.
Note that the weight of the magnet has been reduced by removing yoke material where the ﬁeld is low without compromising the magnet efﬁciency too much. Doing so allows to remove material at the top and bottom corners and outer central region of yoke to reach 85 tons as required by FAIR for the fully assembled magnet.
B. Conductor and Magnet Protection
The conductor is the same as for the Super-FRS dipoles (see Table III of [15] for detailed parameters): it is a NbTi wire (strand) in a copper channel (WiC) with a copper to superconductor ratio of about 11. At nominal current, (228 A), the peak ﬁeld on the conductor is less than 1.45 T and is located on the inner part of the long side (y = 686.5 mm), close to the horizontal tie-rod openings. While plotting the critical curve using the Bottura ﬁt [17] and inserting the operation point (Inom = 228 A; Bpeak = 1.45 T), the margin on the load line is less than 64 % (Icrit = 642 A, Bcrit = 4.1 T) and the temperature margin is a little greater than 3 K (see Fig. 6).
Magnet protection analysis was performed as described in Section III-C of [15] using QTransit, a quench analysis software developed at CEA-Irfu [18] but with different input parameters. Apart from the coil geometry (longer perimeter), the main modiﬁcation comes from that each EB dipole is single powered and not in triplet as it was assumed for the Super-FRS dipoles preliminary studies. Consequently, even in the worst case of

quench detection failure in which 100% of the stored energy, which is 0.83 MJ at 300 A (maximum current), is dissipated in a single coil, both the hot spot (115 K) and the maximum voltage to ground (420V) remain reasonable. Note that the worst case for the voltage to ground arises during normal operation of quench detection for which it reaches 960 V while 80% of the stored energy is dissipated in the 3.2 Ohm dump resistor.
C. Electromagnetic Forces
Electromagnetic forces in coils during energization were evaluated at 110 % of the nominal current to evaluate the mechanical behavior of the coil casing and the associated tie-rods. During their energization, the coils are attracted by the yoke and each undergoes a resulting electromagnetic force of 310 kN which is contained by the stainless steel coil casing. Analyzing the equivalent von Mises stresses, they are all below the acceptable levels as the maximum equivalent stresses are 150 MPa for the stainless steel coil casing and the vacuum vessel and 410 MPa for the tie-rods made of titanium.
Electromagnetic forces induced in the thermal shield after a quench were also analysed and displacements and stresses are similar than the ones obtained for the Super-FRS dipoles. Consequently, the thickness of the inner plates of the short and long side of the copper thermal shield should remain 4 mm which is twice thicker than the rest of the plates of the thermal shield.
Moreover, motion of the end chamfers was observed during the energization of the tests of the ﬁrst Super-FRS dipole. The bolting design was not adequate to withstand the longitudinal electromagnetic forces which pulls the end chamfers towards the coils. Analyzing these forces by integrating the Maxwell stresses on the surfaces of the EB dipole chamfers, it shows they could undergo a 60 kN outward longitudinal forces, therefore the bolting and pinning scheme of the chamfers was adapted accordingly.
V. PERSPECTIVES
The design studies from the CEA team are complete and have been written in a conceptual design report (CDR) provided to FAIR-GSI earlier this year. In parallel, CEA has also prepared general assembly drawings and technical speciﬁcations (“builtto-spec”) so that the tender for the three superferric Energy Buncher dipoles can be launched by FAIR-GSI in the near future.

